Z||000010||Capt Fiero||10-06-2001||03:38 AM||||I too have that program and although it is a good tool to see what should help and what shouldnt help.  The numbers can be way off and dont always add up to what a certian mod would equal.  I do recomend the program to anyone that is looking into building a motor, as well as talk to anyone else that has it so you can share dyno files for diffrent parts.  (IE cam files for popular fiero cam's custom cam's, head flow data, turbo data and that kind of stuff)  ||||reg||1
Z||000011||88formula||10-06-2001||01:27 PM||||Stock 3.4 <P>2000 74.839790 196.454453<BR>2500 94.390236 198.219498<BR>3000 117.973602 206.453812<BR>3500 136.811050 205.216568<BR>4000 150.910355 198.069839<BR>4500 159.495956 186.078613<BR>5000 162.414505 170.535233<BR>5500 158.986481 151.759811<BR>6000 148.987030 130.363663<P>Stock 3.4 with 1.6 ratio rocker arms<BR>2000 74.903748 196.622345<BR>2500 94.519943 198.491882<BR>3000 118.180389 206.815674<BR>3500 137.152267 205.728409<BR>4000 151.393005 198.703323<BR>4500 160.205292 186.906174<BR>5000 163.340408 171.507431<BR>5500 160.174255 152.893600<BR>6000 150.347321 131.553909<P>In a 5-speed Formula with a total weight of 2975 lbs. the stock 3.4 would go 0-60 mph in 6.801 seconds and run the quarter mile in 14.849 seconds @ 93.2 mph. With the 1.6 ratio rockers the Formula would go 0-60 mph in 6.77 seconds and run the quarter mile in 14.823 seconds @ 93.4 mph.<P>The 3.4 with the Crane 2030 and 1.6 rocker arms would go 0-60 mph in 6.749 seconds and run the quarter mile in 14.789 seconds @ 93.3 mph. <P>The 3.4 with the Crane powerMax would go 0-60 mph in 6.774 seconds and run the quarter mile in 14.818 seconds @ 93.2 mph. <P>I used a launch rpm of 2000 and shifted all gears @ 6000 rpm. Messing around with the launch and shift rpm points for each camshaft produces better results. For instance shifting @ 5600 rpm with the Crane powerMax lowers the ET and 0-60 times because that engine runs out of breath @ 6000 rpm. It runs out of breath at higher rpm and makes more mid ranged power because it has a lobe separation angle of 112 degrees as opposed the stock 3.4 can with 106 degrees. The lobe separation angle with the 2030 camshaft is 109 degrees. With 112 degrees the power builds quickly and then falls off quickly. With less separation the power band is spread out more evenly throughout the rpm band and is less peaky. <P>I have the same camshaft as the Crane PowerMax in my 3.4 and the power builds very quickly, peaks @ 4000 rpm and then falls off rapidly after that.   <P>||||reg||1
